# tools/cr/crypto_mldsa_verify_witness.rish -- the ML-DSA verification rung: FIPS 204 ML-DSA.Verify_internal, authored in pure Rye and proven by a doubled
# oracle against Zig's independent std.crypto sign.mldsa Signature.verify. This is the ML-DSA ladder's closing stone -- it composes the GREEN base ring
# (crypto/mldsa_ring.rye), serialization and rounding (crypto/mldsa_encode.rye), samplers (crypto/mldsa_sample.rye), and SHAKE (crypto/shake.rye), decodes a
# public key and a signature, and returns the one verdict -- accept or reject -- a second, independent implementation confirms on every input (Season G).
#
# Season: crypto -- the ML-DSA ladder's verification rung, the door a keeper's peer reads a Kumara identity's post-quantum name through once the old curves
# fall. The verify function holds no key: every seed, message, and signature is a public test vector. Every ML-DSA signature a keeper's name will one day
# carry is checked by exactly this composition, so this witness proves the whole path agrees with the NIST-standard reference on accept AND on reject.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-161537_post-quantum-mlkem-mldsa-pivot.md ("the ML-DSA ladder ... mldsa_sign.rye/mldsa_verify.rye ... proven against
# Zig's std.crypto ML-DSA-44/65/87 and the FIPS-204 KAT. Its front door is the post-quantum successor to Kumara's Ed25519 identity").
# Rye-first spine: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md.
# Clean-room: the ML-DSA.Verify algorithm, the tr/mu hashing, the SampleInBall/UseHint/w1Encode wiring, and the commitment equality are the public NIST
# standard's own; the arithmetic is our own Rye composed over our own GREEN rungs, with Zig's std.crypto read only as the reference to diff against -- never a
# copied line (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/mldsa_verify.rye proves itself by a DOUBLED oracle -- the discipline the whole library rests on. A verifier's answer is a boolean, so parity means
# AGREEING with a second, independent implementation on that verdict for every input: accept the genuine signature Zig's deterministic signer produced,
# reject every single-byte mutation of the commitment, response, hint, message, and public key. FIPS 204 specifies standard-domain arithmetic while Zig's
# std.crypto computes in Montgomery domain, yet both normalize to the canonical residue before UseHint and w1Encode, so the two agree on the recomputed
# commitment exactly. This rung diffs our pure-Rye verdict against Zig's Signature.verify at all three parameter sets over five deterministic seeds and two
# messages each. Purely local -- no key held, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_mldsa_verify_witness.rish

say "crypto-mldsa-verify: Language EN — the ML-DSA verification rung: FIPS 204 ML-DSA.Verify_internal. It decodes pk = rho then BitPack(t1) and sigma = c~ then BitPack(z) then HintBitPack(h), rejecting a malformed hint or an over-large z outright; recomputes tr = H(pk) and mu = H(tr then 0x00 0x00 then M); draws the sparse challenge c = SampleInBall(c~); rebuilds w'_approx = A*z minus c*t1*2^d in the ring; recovers w1' = UseHint(h, w'_approx); and accepts iff the z-norm bound holds AND c~' = H(mu then w1Encode(w1')) equals the signature's c~. This is the ML-DSA ladder's closing stone, composing the GREEN ring, encode, sample, and shake rungs — the door a keeper's peer reads a Kumara identity's post-quantum name through once the old curves fall."
say "crypto-mldsa-verify: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively as verdict parity. A verifier's answer is a single boolean, so parity means agreeing with a second, independent implementation on that answer for every input: accept the genuine signature, reject every tampered one. FIPS 204 specifies standard-domain arithmetic; Zig's std.crypto computes in Montgomery domain, yet both normalize to the canonical residue before UseHint and w1Encode, so the two agree on the recomputed commitment. Our pure-Rye verdict must equal Zig's at every parameter set, on accept and on reject alike. Two strangers confirming one verdict is the discipline the whole library rests on."
say "crypto-mldsa-verify: Lens TAME — bounded loops (k rows, l columns, 256 coefficients, at most omega hint positions), contract asserts on every function, canonical residues in [0, q) throughout, the centered infinity norm measured exactly as ML-DSA's z bound uses it, a malformed hint refused before any arithmetic. Correctness proven by a DOUBLED oracle: our standard-domain verify versus Zig's independent Montgomery-domain std.crypto sign.mldsa Signature.verify, agreeing on the verdict at ML-DSA-44 (k=4 l=4 omega=80), ML-DSA-65 (k=6 l=5 omega=55), and ML-DSA-87 (k=8 l=7 omega=75) over 5 deterministic seeds and 2 messages each — accepting the genuine signature and rejecting single-byte mutations of the commitment, response, hint, message, and public key, a trivial all-zero reference refused. The verify function calls no std.crypto; only the selftest invokes Zig as the parity oracle. Every seed, message, and signature is a public test vector — no key held, no network, no funds, no device; holding or signing with the maintainer's own post-quantum key stays the custody gate, constant-time timing-safety the named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/mldsa_verify"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/mldsa_verify.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-mldsa-verify: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/mldsa_verify.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-mldsa-verify: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-mldsa-verify: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-mldsa-verify' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-mldsa-verify: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-mldsa-verify line"
